{"id": "sevt_01VphLajJREx1EKA9TQuXajV", "content": [{"text": "**Evidence**\n- Checkout deployed `7bd6579` at 09:27:31. Checkout p99 went from about 75 ms at 09:26 to 3,548 ms at 09:27, so the gap is under a minute. It kept climbing to about 5,000 ms by 09:50.\n- The other deploys don't line up. Cart (`63af5b8`) went out at 05:41 and auth (`5e06a12`) at 08:52.\n- The diff replaces one batched `order_id__in` query plus an in-memory grouping with a loop that runs one `OrderItem` query per open order. That is an N+1 query.\n- The log agrees. Per-row `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries first appear at 09:27:32, one second after the deploy, and there are 3,486 of them. They come in bursts under a single request_id, at roughly 4\u201318 ms each. The 115 `order_id IN` matches in the log were not checked against the deploy time.\n- I didn't check `db_pool_utilization` across services. The deploy and the log already explain the spike.\n\n**Fix:** Revert `7bd6579`, or restore the batched query and grouping and keep the explicit loop for readability.\n\n> **Root cause:** `7bd6579` \u2014 \"simplify order summary assembly\" turned the batched order-items query into one query per order (N+1) in `CheckoutSummaryHandler.build_summary`, so checkout latency scales with the number of open orders.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:54:48.652526Z", "type": "agent.message"}
